## User preferences

- when the user asked, “What would you research given this codebase” -> ground research ideas in the implementation already present, identify what is already implemented, and distinguish research gaps from missing basic functionality [Task 1]

## Reusable knowledge

- The highest-value direction is cost-aware evolutionary search under duplicates, noise, variable latency, batching, and failures: compare how GA, MAP-Elites, and NSGA-II allocate evaluations when candidates are duplicated, noisy, slow, batched, or fail [Task 1]
- The key empirical gap is a shared evaluation layer: unique-vs-total budgets, cache policies, repeated sampling, batch/asynchronous execution, timeouts, failures/retries, duration/utilization telemetry, checkpoint/resume, and operator/parent provenance [Task 1]
- Current asymmetries that motivate it: the classical GA evaluates every population each generation and can reevaluate copied elites; MAP-Elites has a `batch_size` loop but calls its scalar evaluator serially; NSGA-II lacks shared evaluation budgets, caching, batching, and async execution [Task 1]
- Use IOHprofiler PBO/W-model/BBOB, COCO noisy/constrained/large-scale/bi-objective suites, QD-suite descriptor-bias/plateau/evolvability-trap tasks, plus synthetic latency/noise/failure-correlated evaluators. Compare paired initial populations and fixed seeds; report anytime curves, evaluation counts, wall time, dispersion/uncertainty, cache-hit rate, utilization, and reevaluated final performance [Task 1]
- `research/STATE_OF_THE_ART.md` contains a 36-paper dossier whose claims were not independently replicated; treat frontier results as hypotheses and prioritize reproducible comparative experiments [Task 1]

## Failures and how to do differently

- Symptom: an attractive new optimizer is proposed before evaluation realism is trustworthy -> cause: the current library needs safe execution, evaluator failure handling, reproducibility, and validation-set fitness first -> fix: do not begin with LLM-driven program evolution; establish the shared evaluation infrastructure and credible experiments first [Task 1]
- Symptom: a comparison looks good on OneMax or one TSP instance -> cause: narrow, non-paired evaluation hides seed and wall-time effects -> fix: use multiple fixed seeds, paired initial populations, anytime curves, evaluation-count and wall-time accounting, and dispersion/uncertainty reporting [Task 1]

